✅ char Primitive Data Type in Java
In Java, char is one of the 8 primitive data types and is used to store a single 16-bit Unicode character.

means 2 byte unicode that support 65535 characters, but other languages that are using 1 byte they uses ASCII that only support 256 characters

🔹 Syntax

char letter = 'A';
🔹 Key Features
| Feature       | Value                                        |
| ------------- | -------------------------------------------- |
| Size          | 2 bytes (16 bits)                            |
| Default value | `\u0000` (null character)                    |
| Range         | `\u0000` (0) to `\uffff` (65,535)            |
| Type          | Unsigned                                     |
| Uses          | Represents characters, digits, symbols, etc. |


🔹 Examples

public class CharExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        char letter = 'A';
        char numberChar = '7';
        char unicodeChar = '\u0905'; // Unicode for Hindi letter अ
        char symbol = '#';

        System.out.println("Letter: " + letter);
        System.out.println("Number Char: " + numberChar);
        System.out.println("Unicode Char: " + unicodeChar);
        System.out.println("Symbol: " + symbol);
    }
}
🔹 char vs int
You can typecast between char and int:

char c = 'A';
int ascii = (int) c; // 65

int num = 66;
char ch = (char) num; // 'B'
🔹 Unicode Support
Since Java uses Unicode, char can represent non-English characters, like:


char urdu = '\u0627'; // ا
char chinese = '\u4E2D'; // 中

✅ Summary
char is a primitive data type that holds a single character.

It supports Unicode, allowing storage of global language symbols.

It is not the same as a String, which holds multiple characters.